                "quote": "if applied >= fine.Amount then \"paid\" elif applied = 0m then \"unpaid\" else $\"paid {money applied} of {money fine.Amount}\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 288,
                "quote": "|> List.filter (fun ((_, was), (fine, now)) -> was < fine.Amount && now >= fine.Amount)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The oldest-first allocation rule is in Tier 2 (Accounts.allocations), and Tier 3 calls it. Tier 3 then re-derives domain meaning on top of it. It decides when a fine counts as paid, unpaid or part-paid. It also decides which fines a payment settled, by diffing allocations before and after with its own predicate. Neither contradicts Tier 2, but Tier 3 is re-deciding fine status rather than asking Tier 2.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The change adds no clock, I/O, persistence or console access. Accounts.allocations is pure. The Views and CLI changes only compute and format values. No port bypass and no Tier 4 re-entry into Tier 2.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 271,
                "quote": "if applied >= fine.Amount then \"paid\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 288,
                "quote": "was < fine.Amount && now >= fine.Amount"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The fully-paid predicate (applied against fine.Amount) is written twice in Tier 3, in the account status and in the settled computation. Tier 2 owns the allocation but exposes no single 'fine is paid' definition. The copies agree today but could drift.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 277,
                "quote": "| Some w when applied = 0m -> $\"waived by {w.Approver}\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 280,
                "quote": "| None when applied >= fine.Amount -> \"paid\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 301,
                "quote": "Approver = approver.Trim()"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The waiver rules live in Tier 1/2: the Waiver type, Waivers.waive, and Accounts.allocations and owed. The change also replaces the inline borrow-block arithmetic in the CLI with a Tier 2 call, which removes a Tier 4 duplicate of a rule. Tier 3 still derives the per-fine status (waived, rest waived, paid, unpaid) with its own conditions. ofWaiver repeats the approver Trim that Tier 2 applies. The copies agree with Tier 2.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Host/Cli/CommandLine.fs",
                "line": 164,
                "quote": "if Accounts.isBlocked (MemberId m) library then"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The new code adds no effects below Tier 4. The waive command goes through UseCases.waive and the commit/Store port, and JsonCodec encodes the waiver in the adapter. But the CLI (presentation) now calls a Tier 2 function, Accounts.isBlocked, directly and makes the borrow-block decision itself. The rubric scores Tier 4 presentation re-entering Tier 2 decisions directly as 0 (ARC-BND-02). The change replaces a worse inline decision, and the decision belongs in a UseCase. The call is still a new presentation-to-Tier-2 edge.",
            "score": 0
          },
